# The typed half of the shell: redirection, paths, job control and the history
# expansions. No check: field — this is the shell itself, always here.
name:  shell
what:  the syntax you meet in every other tool's examples
docs:  man zshexpn, man bash (EXPANSION)

= PLUMBING — reading other people's commands
\|           | send this command's output into the next one
>  file      | write output to file, replacing it
>> file      | append output to file
2> file      | write errors to file, leaving normal output alone
2>&1         | fold errors into normal output, so \| catches both
< file       | feed file in as input
cmd \| tee f | show output AND save it to f
$(cmd)       | substitute what cmd printed, right here

= PATHS
~            | your home directory
.            | here
..           | one directory up
cd -         | back to the directory you were in before
*            | any run of characters in a filename
**/          | this directory and every directory under it

= JOBS — the typed half of Ctrl-z
jobs         | what you have suspended or backgrounded
fg           | resume the last one in the foreground
bg           | resume it in the background, prompt stays yours
kill %1      | kill job 1 from that list
cmd &        | start it in the background from the outset

= HISTORY YOU CAN TYPE
history      | print the lot; `history \| grep podman` to hunt
!!           | the whole previous command. `sudo !!` is the classic
!$           | the last argument of the previous command
!abc         | the most recent command starting with abc

= YOUR OWN ALIASES
# Read out of the running shell, so this group is whatever your rc files
# actually define — including the ones this repo adds. The description is the
# expansion, unless the row below gives one.
probe:   shell-alias
@cc                  | Claude Code, one word
@ccc                 | continue the most recent conversation
@ccr                 | pick an older session from a list
@cx                  | Codex, one word
@cxr                 | continue the most recent
@cxrr                | pick an older session from a list

# A shell FUNCTION, not an alias, so `alias` does not report it and the probe
# cannot see it. Literal on purpose.
omniroute-where      | which gateway and which combos, before you debug anything
